from tnsoc import *
S(10,10,"Asha traced India's shape on a map. \"Mountains in the north, sea on three sides,\" she said.",
 ["India lies in the northern and eastern hemispheres between 8 degrees 4 minutes and 37 degrees 6 minutes north latitude. The Tropic of Cancer passes through the middle. The Standard Meridian is 82.5 degrees east, which gives Indian Standard Time.",
  "India's physical divisions are the Northern Mountains (Himalayas), the Northern Plains, the Peninsular Plateau, the Indian Desert, the Coastal Plains and the Islands. The Western Ghats and Eastern Ghats meet at the Nilgiris, and Anaimudi is the highest peak in South India.",
  "The Himalayan rivers such as the Ganga, Indus and Brahmaputra are perennial. Peninsular rivers like the Godavari, Krishna and Cauvery are seasonal, and they flow east into the Bay of Bengal, except the Narmada and Tapi, which flow west."],
 ["Tropic of Cancer","Standard Meridian","Himalayas","Western Ghats","peninsular rivers","Deccan Plateau"],
 ("Why are Himalayan rivers perennial?",["They are fed by melting snow.","They also get monsoon rain.","So they flow all year."]),
 ("The Narmada flows into the Bay of Bengal.",["It flows west into the Arabian Sea.","Most peninsular rivers flow east."]),
 ["India's Standard Meridian: 82.5 E.","Physical divisions: mountains, plains, plateau, desert, coast, islands.","Himalayan rivers are perennial.","Peninsular rivers are seasonal."],
 ["m|The Tropic of Cancer passes through|middle India|south India|Himalayas only|23.5 N.","t|The Ganga is a Himalayan river.|T|Yes.","m|The Standard Meridian is|82.5 E|90 E|75 E|IST.","t|The Cauvery flows west.|F|East.","m|The highest peak of South India is|Anaimudi|Everest|Doddabetta|Western Ghats.","s|Which mountains lie in northern India?|himalayas|Northern mountains."])
S(10,11,"Raju felt a hot summer wind. \"What decides India's climate?\" he asked.",
 ["India has a tropical monsoon climate with four seasons: winter, summer, the south-west monsoon and the retreating north-east monsoon. Factors affecting it are latitude, altitude, distance from the sea, the monsoon winds and relief.",
  "The south-west monsoon brings most of India's rain between June and September. Tamil Nadu gets much of its rain from the north-east monsoon in October to December. Cyclones form in the Bay of Bengal.",
  "Natural vegetation includes tropical evergreen, deciduous, thorn, mountain and mangrove forests. Mangroves grow in deltas such as the Sundarbans, while the Western Ghats have evergreen forests. Forests are conserved to protect wildlife."],
 ["monsoon","south-west monsoon","north-east monsoon","tropical evergreen","mangrove","deciduous forest"],
 ("Why does Tamil Nadu get rain in winter?",["North-east monsoon winds blow from the sea.","They bring rain in Oct to Dec.","So the state is wet then."]),
 ("The monsoon brings rain only from December.",["The south-west monsoon is June to September.","North-east is October to December."]),
 ["Tropical monsoon climate.","Four seasons.","Tamil Nadu: north-east monsoon.","Forests: evergreen, deciduous, thorn, mangrove."],
 ["m|The south-west monsoon is from|June to September|Dec to Feb|March to May|Main rainy season.","t|Mangroves grow in deltas.|T|Yes.","m|Tamil Nadu gets rain mainly from the|north-east monsoon|south-west monsoon|snow|Oct-Dec.","t|India has a desert climate everywhere.|F|Tropical monsoon.","m|The Sundarbans have|mangroves|thorn forest|tundra|Coastal.","s|How many seasons are in India's classification?|4|Winter, summer, monsoon, retreating."])
S(10,12,"Meena saw paddy and wheat on a food table. \"Where do these grow?\" she asked.",
 ["Agriculture is the backbone of Indian economy. The main seasons are Kharif (June to October), Rabi (October to March) and Zaid. Rice is a Kharif crop and wheat is a Rabi crop.",
  "Major crops are rice, wheat, millets, pulses, sugarcane, cotton, jute, tea and coffee. Rice grows in the wet plains and deltas, wheat in the Ganga plains, tea in Assam and the Nilgiris and cotton in black soil regions.",
  "Soils include alluvial, black, red, laterite and desert soils. Irrigation by canals, tanks and wells supports farming. The Green Revolution brought high-yielding seeds, and issues like soil erosion need soil conservation."],
 ["Kharif","Rabi","alluvial soil","black soil","irrigation","Green Revolution"],
 ("Why is cotton grown in black soil?",["Black soil holds moisture.","It is fertile.","Cotton needs such soil."]),
 ("Wheat is a Kharif crop.",["Wheat is a Rabi crop.","Rice is Kharif."]),
 ["Kharif: June-October.","Rabi: October-March.","Alluvial soil is fertile.","Black soil suits cotton."],
 ["m|Rice is a|Kharif crop|Rabi crop|Zaid crop|Monsoon crop.","t|Wheat is grown in the Rabi season.|T|Yes.","m|Cotton grows well in|black soil|desert soil|sand|Moisture-retaining.","t|Tea is grown in Assam.|T|Yes.","m|The most fertile soil is|alluvial|laterite|desert|River-made.","s|Which season is June to October?|kharif|Monsoon crops."])
S(10,13,"Raju read that a steel plant uses coal and iron. \"Where do these come from?\" he asked.",
 ["Resources are things from nature that people use. Minerals include iron ore, coal, bauxite and mica, and power resources include coal, petroleum, natural gas, water and the sun and wind. Conventional resources like coal are limited, while non-conventional resources, such as solar and wind, are renewable.",
  "Industries are classed as agro-based (cotton textile, sugar), mineral-based (iron and steel) and others such as cement and IT. Industries cluster near raw material, power and markets.",
  "Tamil Nadu is known for cotton textiles at Coimbatore and Tiruppur, automobile industries around Chennai and leather at Vellore. Mines like lignite at Neyveli feed power plants."],
 ["resource","conventional energy","non-conventional energy","iron and steel","agro-based industry","Neyveli"],
 ("Why are solar and wind called renewable?",["They are not used up.","They come from nature again and again.","They cause little pollution."]),
 ("Coal is a renewable resource.",["Coal is limited.","Solar and wind are renewable."]),
 ["Minerals and power resources.","Renewable and non-renewable energy.","Agro-based and mineral-based industry.","Tamil Nadu: textiles and automobiles."],
 ["m|A renewable energy source is|solar|coal|petroleum|Not used up.","t|Neyveli has lignite.|T|Yes.","m|Cotton textile is an|agro-based industry|mineral-based industry|mining|Uses cotton.","t|Iron ore is a power resource.|F|It is a mineral.","m|The automobile hub of the south is|Chennai|Madurai|Salem|Many plants.","s|Which resource is solar and wind: renewable or not?|renewable|Yes."])
S(10,14,"Asha saw a crowded train station. \"How does India move so many people and goods?\" she asked.",
 ["India's population is over a billion, spread unevenly, with the Ganga plains very dense. Density is the number of people per square km. Literacy and sex ratio are important measures.",
  "Transport includes roads, railways, waterways and airways. The Golden Quadrilateral links the four metros, railways are the lifeline, and ports like Chennai and Mumbai handle sea trade. Communication includes the post, telephone, radio, TV and the internet.",
  "Trade is the exchange of goods. India exports items such as textiles, IT services and engineering goods and imports petroleum and gold. The balance of trade is the difference between exports and imports."],
 ["population density","sex ratio","Golden Quadrilateral","port","export","balance of trade"],
 ("Why are railways called the lifeline?",["They carry many passengers and goods.","They are cheap over long distances.","They link all parts of India."]),
 ("Exports are goods bought from other countries.",["Exports are sold abroad.","Imports are bought."]),
 ["Population is unevenly spread.","Four modes of transport.","Golden Quadrilateral links metros.","Balance of trade = exports minus imports."],
 ["m|Density means people per|square km|house|village|Population measure.","t|Chennai is a major port.|T|Yes.","m|India's lifeline transport is|railways|airways|pipelines|Long distances.","t|Imports are sold to other countries.|F|Exports are.","m|The Golden Quadrilateral links the|four metros|villages|ports only|Highways.","s|What do we call goods bought from abroad?|imports|Opposite of exports."])
S(10,15,"Meena looked at the map of Tamil Nadu. \"Hills, plains and a long coast,\" she said.",
 ["Tamil Nadu lies in the south-east of India, bordered by the Bay of Bengal and the Indian Ocean. Its relief includes the Western Ghats in the west, the Eastern Ghats and plateau regions, the central plains and the Coromandel coastal plain.",
  "The major rivers are the Cauvery, Vaigai, Tamirabarani, Palar and Pennaiyar. The Cauvery delta is the most fertile. Tamil Nadu's soils are red, black, alluvial and laterite.",
  "The climate is tropical with a hot summer and two monsoons. Forests are found on the hills, and the Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve, Mudumalai and Anamalai protect wildlife. Palk Bay and the Gulf of Mannar are coastal wonders."],
 ["Coromandel Coast","Cauvery","Western Ghats","Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve","Gulf of Mannar","red soil"],
 ("Why is the Cauvery delta fertile?",["The river brings silt.","The soil is alluvial.","It has water for farming."]),
 ("Tamil Nadu has no coastline.",["It has a long coast.","The Coromandel coast lies on the east."]),
 ["South-east India.","Coromandel coastal plain.","Cauvery, Vaigai, Tamirabarani.","Biosphere reserves in hills."],
 ["m|The east coast of Tamil Nadu is the|Coromandel Coast|Malabar Coast|Konkan|Coastal plain.","t|The Cauvery is a major river.|T|Yes.","m|The Gulf of Mannar is in the|south-east|north|west|Marine biosphere.","t|Tamil Nadu has only hills.|F|Hills, plains, coast.","m|A hill reserve in Tamil Nadu is|Nilgiri|Sundarbans|Kaziranga|Biosphere reserve.","s|Which delta is the rice bowl?|cauvery|Fertile."])
S(10,16,"Raju noticed the busy city of Chennai. \"How do people live in different parts of Tamil Nadu?\" he asked.",
 ["Human geography studies people and their activities. Tamil Nadu has a large population with high urbanisation, and Chennai, Coimbatore and Madurai are big cities. Literacy is high and the sex ratio is better than the national average.",
  "Farming, fishing, industry and services are the main occupations. Transport includes a strong road and rail network, ports at Chennai, Ennore and Thoothukudi and airports at Chennai, Madurai and Coimbatore.",
  "Tamil Nadu is known for its temples, handlooms, festivals and arts. Tourism, IT and education add to the economy, and we should preserve its heritage and the environment."],
 ["human geography","urbanisation","literacy","ports","tourism","heritage"],
 ("Why is Tamil Nadu highly urbanised?",["Industry and services in cities.","Migration from villages.","Better jobs and schools."]),
 ("Tamil Nadu has no ports.",["It has ports at Chennai, Ennore and Thoothukudi.","Ports aid trade."]),
 ["Large urban population.","Occupations: farming, fishing, industry, services.","Good transport.","Rich heritage."],
 ["m|A major port in Tamil Nadu is|Chennai|Delhi|Bhopal|On the coast.","t|Tamil Nadu is highly urbanised.|T|Yes.","m|Studying people and their activities is|human geography|physical geography|geology|Human focus.","t|Tourism does not help the economy.|F|It does.","m|Tamil Nadu is famous for|temples and handlooms|glaciers|deserts|Heritage.","s|Name a large city in Tamil Nadu.|chennai|Or Madurai."])
S(10,17,"Asha's teacher held up a thin book. \"This guides the whole country,\" she said.",
 ["The Constitution of India is the supreme law. It was drafted by the Constituent Assembly, with Dr B. R. Ambedkar as chairman of the Drafting Committee, adopted on 26 November 1949 and came into force on 26 January 1950, celebrated as Republic Day.",
  "The Preamble declares India a sovereign, socialist, secular, democratic republic and promises justice, liberty, equality and fraternity. The Fundamental Rights are guaranteed by the Constitution, and Directive Principles guide the government.",
  "Fundamental Duties remind citizens of their responsibilities. The Constitution can be amended, and the basic structure cannot be changed. India has a federal structure with a strong centre."],
 ["Constitution","Constituent Assembly","Preamble","Fundamental Rights","Directive Principles","Fundamental Duties"],
 ("What does the Preamble say?",["India is sovereign, socialist, secular, democratic and a republic.","It promises justice, liberty, equality, fraternity.","It gives the aims of the Constitution."]),
 ("The Constitution came into force on 15 August 1947.",["It came into force on 26 January 1950.","15 August 1947 is Independence."]),
 ["Ambedkar chaired the Drafting Committee.","Constitution in force 26 Jan 1950.","Preamble gives ideals.","Rights, principles and duties."],
 ["m|The Constitution came into force in|1950|1947|1935|Republic Day.","t|Ambedkar chaired the Drafting Committee.|T|Yes.","m|The Preamble begins with|We, the people of India|India is a republic|Rights|Constitution opening.","t|Fundamental Duties are in the Constitution.|T|Yes.","m|Which guides the government?|Directive Principles|Taxes|Tradition|Not enforceable.","s|On what date is Republic Day?|26 january|1950."])
S(10,18,"Meena watched the Prime Minister on TV. \"What does the Prime Minister do?\" she asked.",
 ["The Union government has three organs. The legislature is Parliament, consisting of the President, the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha. The Lok Sabha is elected by the people for five years and the Rajya Sabha is a permanent house whose members retire by rotation.",
  "The President is the head of state and acts on the advice of the Council of Ministers. The Prime Minister is the head of government, leads the Council of Ministers and is responsible to the Lok Sabha.",
  "The Supreme Court is the highest court and protects the Constitution. The judiciary is independent and can review laws. Parliament makes laws, passes the budget and watches the executive."],
 ["Parliament","Lok Sabha","Rajya Sabha","President","Prime Minister","Supreme Court"],
 ("Who is the head of government in India?",["The Prime Minister.","Leader of the Council of Ministers.","Responsible to the Lok Sabha."]),
 ("The Rajya Sabha is dissolved every five years.",["It is a permanent house.","The Lok Sabha is elected for five years."]),
 ["Parliament: President, Lok Sabha, Rajya Sabha.","President is head of state.","PM is head of government.","Supreme Court protects Constitution."],
 ["m|The head of state is the|President|Prime Minister|Chief Justice|Constitutional head.","t|The Lok Sabha is elected by people.|T|Yes.","m|The highest court is the|Supreme Court|High Court|District Court|Apex.","t|The Rajya Sabha is dissolved.|F|It is permanent.","m|The PM leads the|Council of Ministers|Supreme Court|Army|Executive.","s|How long is the Lok Sabha term?|5|Five years."])
S(10,19,"Raju asked who is the head of his state. \"The Governor and the Chief Minister,\" said his teacher.",
 ["Each state has a legislature, an executive and a judiciary. The Governor is the head of state, appointed by the President. The Chief Minister is the head of government and leads the Council of Ministers responsible to the Legislative Assembly.",
  "The Legislative Assembly (Vidhan Sabha) is elected by people for five years. Some states, like Tamil Nadu earlier, had a Legislative Council. The Assembly makes laws on State List subjects such as police, public health and agriculture.",
  "The High Court is the highest court of the state, and the Madras High Court serves Tamil Nadu. Local bodies and the state work together."],
 ["Governor","Chief Minister","Legislative Assembly","State List","High Court","Council of Ministers"],
 ("What does the Chief Minister do?",["Heads the state government.","Leads the Council of Ministers.","Is responsible to the Assembly."]),
 ("The Governor is elected by the people.",["The President appoints the Governor.","The Assembly members are elected."]),
 ["Governor is head of state.","CM is head of government.","Assembly is elected.","High Court is the state's top court."],
 ["m|The head of a state government is the|Chief Minister|Governor|President|Executive.","t|The Governor is appointed by the President.|T|Yes.","m|The state law-making house is the|Legislative Assembly|Lok Sabha|Supreme Court|Vidhan Sabha.","t|MLAs are elected.|T|Yes.","m|The Madras High Court serves|Tamil Nadu|Kerala|Karnataka|Chennai.","s|How long is the Assembly term?|5|Five years."])
S(10,20,"Asha asked how India talks with other countries. \"Through foreign policy,\" said the teacher.",
 ["Foreign policy is how a country deals with others. India's foreign policy is guided by the principles of peace, non-alignment, respect for sovereignty and cooperation. Jawaharlal Nehru shaped its early form with the Panchsheel principles.",
  "India follows the Non-Aligned Movement, supports the United Nations and works for disarmament and against colonialism and apartheid. It has good relations with neighbours and the world and believes in 'the world is one family'.",
  "India stays strong with an independent policy, and its relations with major powers and its neighbours matter for trade and peace. The Ministry of External Affairs carries out the foreign policy."],
 ["foreign policy","non-alignment","Panchsheel","sovereignty","disarmament","Ministry of External Affairs"],
 ("What is non-alignment?",["Not joining any power bloc.","Taking independent decisions.","India is a founder."]),
 ("India supported colonialism.",["India opposed colonialism.","It supported freedom movements."]),
 ["Foreign policy: dealing with others.","Panchsheel by Nehru.","Non-alignment.","Support for the UN."],
 ["m|Panchsheel was shaped by|Nehru|Gandhi|Patel|Five principles.","t|India supports the UN.|T|Yes.","m|Non-alignment means|staying out of blocs|joining NATO|trade only|Independent.","t|India opposed apartheid.|T|Yes.","m|Foreign policy is handled by the|Ministry of External Affairs|Finance Ministry|Army|MEA.","s|How many Panchsheel principles are there?|5|Five."])
S(10,21,"Meena read about India's neighbours on a map. \"Who are our neighbours?\" she asked.",
 ["India has several neighbours: Pakistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Myanmar, Sri Lanka and the Maldives. Relations include trade, culture and sometimes border disputes. India works for peaceful solutions.",
  "SAARC, formed in 1985, is the South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation. India also joins groups like BRICS, the G20 and the Commonwealth, and plays a role in the UN, including in peacekeeping.",
  "India's relations with big powers like the USA, Russia and the European Union matter for trade, defence and technology. India also helps other countries through aid and education."],
 ["SAARC","BRICS","G20","border dispute","peacekeeping","bilateral relations"],
 ("Why is SAARC useful?",["It promotes regional cooperation.","It helps trade and culture.","It builds friendship among neighbours."]),
 ("India has no neighbours.",["India has many neighbours.","Pakistan and China are two."]),
 ["India's neighbours are many.","SAARC formed in 1985.","India joins BRICS and G20.","India helps in UN peacekeeping."],
 ["m|SAARC was formed in|1985|1947|1991|South Asia.","t|India is a member of BRICS.|T|Yes.","m|A neighbour of India is|Nepal|Japan|Brazil|Bordering country.","t|India never joins UN peacekeeping.|F|It does.","m|BRICS includes|Brazil, Russia, India, China, South Africa|Only Asia|Only EU|Five nations.","s|SAARC stands for South Asian Association for what?|regional cooperation|Regional Cooperation."])
